<h2>One repair, several names</h2>
<p>Homeowners hear this called pipe lining, relining, epoxy lining, or cured-in-place pipe, usually shortened to CIPP. They all describe the same idea: a flexible tube coated with resin goes into the damaged pipe, gets pressed against the wall, and hardens into a new pipe that follows the old one from end to end.</p>
<p>All Sewer and Plumbing Services uses Perma-Liner equipment and materials. Our owner, Jeff Haug, trained on the system and bought it when lining was still rare in Colorado, and we have been putting it to work on Front Range sewers ever since.</p>

<h2>How the liner goes in</h2>
<p>Every lining job starts with video. We run a camera from the cleanout to the main, measure where each defect sits, and locate the line from the surface so we know its depth and path. Then the pipe is cleaned until the camera shows bare wall, because the liner has to seat against the host pipe rather than against roots, grease or rust scale.</p>
<p>The liner tube is cut to length and wetted out with resin on site. It is then pushed in by air pressure, turning inside out as it travels, or pulled into position and inflated with a bladder. Either way, pressure holds the resin-filled material tight to the old pipe while it cures. When the cure is complete, the bladder comes out and any branch connections are reopened.</p>
<p>What is left is a smooth, seamless pipe with no bell joints every few feet. Those joints are where most Colorado clay and cast iron lines let roots in and where shifting soil pulls sections apart, so removing them removes the most common failure point.</p>

