<h2>What lining is good at</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Clay tile laterals with root intrusion at the joints, once the roots are cut out</li>
  <li>Cast iron that has rusted thin or cracked along the bottom but still holds its round shape</li>
  <li>Hairline and longitudinal cracks that let groundwater in or sewage out</li>
  <li>Modest joint offsets that a liner can bridge smoothly</li>
  <li>Lines under things you would rather not disturb: finished basements, porches, driveways, mature trees, or the street</li>
</ul>
<p>Some of our own jobs show the range. In Lakewood we lined 67 feet of 1950s cast iron whose bottom had rotted out, and finished the same day. In Centennial a 47-foot cast iron line that was flaking apart was lined from a cleanout inside the house. In Lafayette, a 44-foot cast iron line close to collapse was lined so the owners did not have to lose a porch and driveway to an excavator.</p>

<h2>What lining cannot fix</h2>
<p>A cured liner takes the exact shape of the pipe around it. That is the whole trick, and it is also the limit. We will not recommend lining for:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bellies.</strong> Expansive clay often lets a section sink, leaving a low spot that holds water. A liner cured in that spot holds water too.</li>
  <li><strong>Lost grade.</strong> If part of the line now runs flat or uphill toward the main, lining preserves the problem.</li>
  <li><strong>Collapsed or missing pipe.</strong> The liner needs a host wall to form against.</li>
  <li><strong>Large offsets or shifted sections.</strong> Past a certain step, the liner wrinkles or will not pass.</li>
  <li><strong>Badly deformed Orangeburg.</strong> Fiber pipe that has flattened into an oval is a replacement job.</li>
</ul>
<p>Those findings point to <a href="/pipe-bursting/">pipe bursting</a> or to a dig. Often the best plan is a mix: excavate the one bad section as a spot repair, then line the rest. On a job for a builder in Arvada we did exactly that on 36 feet of cast iron, repairing one section and lining through to the city main. If you want to see when a dig makes sense, read our <a href="/sewer-line-replacement/">sewer line replacement page</a>.</p>

<h2>Colorado details that change the plan</h2>
<h3>Depth and frost</h3>
<p>Front Range sewers are laid below frost depth, so reaching the far end with a pit can mean real digging. Lining from an inside cleanout often avoids it entirely, which is why we look hard at existing access before proposing any excavation. In Littleton we lined 72 feet of clay working entirely from inside the house.</p>

<h3>The part under the street</h3>
<p>In most Front Range cities and districts, the owner is responsible for the lateral all the way to the main, including the tap and the section under the road. Lining can often reach that section without a street cut, which saves both the pavement and the right-of-way permit that comes with opening it.</p>

<h3>District rules</h3>
<p>Many suburban and unincorporated neighborhoods are served by special sanitation districts that write their own standards, and some restrict or place conditions on certain lining methods. We confirm the requirements for your address with the city or district before we schedule, so the work is accepted on inspection.</p>

<h2>Service life</h2>
<p>Liner manufacturers, Perma-Liner included, publish their own design-life ratings for their systems, and we are glad to share the product documentation for the liner we install on your job. What we can say from experience is simple: the cured pipe does not rust, has no joints for roots, and does not care about the soil chemistry that eats cast iron. The inside diameter drops slightly, but a smooth wall usually carries flow better than the rough, scaled pipe it replaced.</p>

<h2>Proof at the end</h2>
<p>After the cure we run the camera again, from the house to the main, and give you the recording. You should see a continuous interior with no roots, no open joints and steady fall. Keep it; it is useful when you sell the house. To see how the whole job flows from first call to final video, visit <a href="/our-process/">our process page</a>.</p>
